The implementation is complete and working end-to-end. Let me summarize against the goal and acceptance criteria.

## Summary

Implemented `shipwright pipeline watch [issue]` — a real-time, stage-level progress stream.

**What was built:**
- **`scripts/lib/pipeline-watch.sh`** (new) — read-only polling dashboard over artifacts the pipeline already writes (`pipeline-state.md`, `loop-logs/progress.md`, `events.jsonl`). No new transport. Key functions: snapshot-safe frontmatter reader, portable elapsed/ETA math, stage progress bar, build-stage panel, events activity tail.
- **`scripts/sw-pipeline.sh`** — sources the lib + adds `watch)` dispatch.
- **`scripts/lib/pipeline-cli.sh`** — help command entry + example.
- **`scripts/sw-pipeline-watch-test.sh`** (new) — 28-assertion suite, registered in `package.json`.
- **`.claude/CLAUDE.md`** — command table + test-suites table rows.

**Acceptance criteria — all met:**
| Criterion | Status |
|---|---|
| `watch [issue]` streams, refreshes ~5s | ✓ |
| Current stage, progress bar, elapsed, last-activity | ✓ |
| Build stage: iteration, test pass/fail, commits, token usage | ✓ |
| Clean exit (code 0 + summary) on complete/failed/aborted; Ctrl-C exits 0 + restores cursor | ✓ |
| Works for daemon-spawned & manual pipelines (reads shared state file) | ✓ |
| Existing tests pass | ✓ (watch: 28/28, pipeline-state: 46/46) |

**Edge cases handled:** empty/missing state, missing progress.md, no `jq`, BSD/GNU date portability, stall detection, snapshot temp cleanup on interrupt, non-TTY one-shot mode for tests.

**Note:** `shipwright docs sync` fails early in this environment due to a pre-existing stray artifact (`.claude/pipeline-artifacts/wiki/Pipeline-Design-190.md` containing a placeholder `AUTO:section-id` marker) — unrelated to this change. I updated the affected doc tables manually in the exact format the generator emits.

